WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.860
Consider this four by four matrix.

00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:06.520
Put negative ones on the diagonal
and positive ones everywhere else.

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:12.240
Its rows have a special property: every
row is perpendicular to every other row.

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:17.920
Compare the first two rows. Two positions
disagree and contribute negative one.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:22.580
Two agree and contribute positive
one. Their dot product is zero.

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:25.150
Every row has squared length four,

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:29.020
so H times H transpose equals
four times the identity.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:34.420
A matrix of signs with mutually orthogonal
rows is called a Hadamard matrix.

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:37.880
In dimension n, its defining equation is H

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:41.340
times H transpose equals
n times the identity.

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:45.870
Hadamard matrices exist
in dimensions one and two.

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:50.270
In every larger dimension, the
order must be divisible by four.

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:54.500
The general Hadamard conjecture asks
whether every positive multiple of

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:58.100
four works. Our original
matrix has an extra symmetry.

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:02.600
Shift the first row cyclically one place
to the right, and you get the second row.

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:07.320
Repeat to get the third and fourth. The
entire matrix is determined by one row.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:12.700
Such a matrix is called circulant.
The circulant Hadamard conjecture

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:16.760
says that this extra symmetry
leaves only orders one and four.

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:20.520
We will explain the proposed proof
through algebra and number theory.

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:24.360
At this overview level, two deeper
results are accepted inputs:

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:27.140
the order restriction
and the delta theorem.

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:30.460
The proof is organized
into eight components.

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:34.280
Follow the arrows from each
ingredient to the steps that use it.

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:38.820
First, group ring encoding replaces
the first row by a polynomial.

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:42.380
Multiplication by its cyclic
reversal records every dot product.

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:45.140
Orthogonality becomes
one polynomial equation.

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:49.580
Second, the order restriction
says that, apart from order one,

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:52.940
n is four times u squared, with u odd.

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:56.230
It remains to rule out u greater than one.

00:01:56.600 --> 00:02:02.080
Third, split the cyclic structure into a
part of size four and a part of odd size.

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:06.060
Evaluating the resulting
polynomial at one, negative one,

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:10.940
and i produces three polynomials, c,
d, and g, with the same norm identity.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:15.420
Fourth, the delta theorem supplies
the number theoretic input.

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:19.660
A special product of values at
roots of unity must itself be a

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:24.160
root of unity of odd order. The three
polynomials satisfy its hypotheses.

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:28.920
Fifth, the original signs give
another constraint. Combine c,

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:31.620
d, and g into a new polynomial, E.

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:36.510
Modulo one plus i, every
coefficient of E becomes one.

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:39.340
Sixth, bring the two branches together.

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:44.660
Certain odd order roots of unity are
also congruent to one modulo one plus i.

00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:48.980
Rigidity forces those roots to equal
one, giving exact product relations.

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:52.480
Seventh, turn those products into sums.

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:57.000
A first order expansion shows
that a particular alternating sum,

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:00.800
S, is zero modulo one plus i.

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:03.880
Eighth, use the all ones coefficients.

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:08.060
At nontrivial roots of unity
their contributions cancel.

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:11.120
At one, an odd number of ones survives,

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:14.860
forcing that same sum to
be one modulo one plus i.

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:20.540
The same sum cannot be both zero
and one in this nonzero quotient.

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:25.110
That is the contradiction.
Thus u cannot exceed one.

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:30.000
Together with the trivial case, the
only possible orders are one and four.

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:33.660
Open each node to explore its
argument and the inputs it uses.
